 ## Overview
-Voxel Ray Marching with Traversal Order Optimization renders a 3D voxel texture by marching rays from the camera through the volume, compositing samples using front-to-back alpha blending to visualize the volume [S1, S2, unofficial]. It differs from its parent [Ray-grid traversal](17-ray-grid-traversal.md) by explicitly optimizing the traversal order of voxel boundaries using precomputed time deltas ($t_{\Delta}$) and maximum boundary times ($t_{max}$), minimizing floating-point operations during the loop [S2, unofficial]. Unlike [Sparse Voxel Octree Ray Traversal](2451-sparse-voxel-octree-ray-traversal.md), it operates on a dense uniform grid, and unlike [Hierarchical Trilinear Ray Casting](851-hierarchical-trilinear-ray-casting.md), it does not use hierarchical acceleration structures, relying instead on the efficiency of the DDA-like traversal logic [S2, unofficial].
+Voxel Ray Marching with Traversal Order Optimization renders a 3D voxel texture by marching rays from the camera through the volume, compositing samples with front-to-back alpha blending to visualize the volume [S1, unofficial]. It differs from standard fixed-step ray marching by optimizing the traversal order of voxel boundaries using precomputed time deltas ($t_{\Delta}$) and maximum boundary times ($t_{max}$), minimizing floating-point operations per step [S2, unofficial]. The algorithm advances analytically from one cell boundary to the next, avoiding fixed-distance sampling steps [S5, S7, unofficial].
 
